Selection Sort

Optimal Solution

Approach:
Traverse the array from the first element to the second last element.
Assume the current index is the minimum element.
Search the remaining unsorted part of the array to find the actual minimum element.
If a smaller element is found, update the minimum index.
After the inner loop, swap the current element with the minimum element (only if they are different).
Repeat this process until the array is completely sorted.

Idea: In each iteration, place the smallest element from the unsorted portion at its correct position in the sorted portion.

Time Complexity: O(n²)
For each element, we scan the remaining unsorted part of the array.
Total comparisons = (n−1) + (n−2) + ... + 1 = O(n²).

Space Complexity: O(1)
The sorting is performed in-place without using any extra data structure.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> selectionSort(vector<int>& nums) {

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size() - 1; i++) {

            int min = i;

            for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.size(); j++) {

                if (nums[j] < nums[min]) {
                    min = j;
                }
            }

            if (min != i) {
                swap(nums[min], nums[i]);
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
};


Key Takeaway:

"Find the minimum element → Swap it with the current position → Grow the sorted portion one element at a time."
